{{wikipedia|dab=posthumous}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[post-humous]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la}} {{term|posthumus|lang=la}}, a variant spelling of {{term|postumus|lang=la}}, superlative form of {{term|posterus|lang=la}} ("coming after"), the "h" added by association with {{term|lang=la|humus}} ("ground, earth") referring to burial.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈpɒs.tʃə.məs/|/ˈpɒs.tʃʊ.məs/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{originally}} Born after the death of one's father.
#: '''''Posthumous''' orphans never even knew their fathers.
# After the [[death]] of someone
#: The '''posthumous''' [[:simple:Medal of Honor|Medal of Honor]] was given to the family of the soldier who died in battle.
# Taking place after one's own death
#: ''Artists obscure during their life often receive '''posthumous''' recognition, too late for them to enjoy.''
# In reference to a [[work]], published after the [[author]]'s death.
#: ''His memoirs were his '''posthumous''' revenge on enemies he dared not take on alive.

====Derived terms====
* [[posthumous execution]]
* [[posthumously]]
* [[posthumousness]]
* [[posthumous work]]

